<% content_for :header_tags do %>
  <%= stylesheet_link_tag 'redmine_reporter_dashboards', plugin: 'redmine_reporter_dashboards' %>
<% end %>

<%#
  T-23 — one report, rendered.

  The body goes through `reporter_report_frame`, which puts it in an opaque-origin
  sandbox — `technical-spec.md` §4, INV-9's third mechanism. It is NOT markup in this
  page and must never become markup in this page: a template is written by an ordinary
  project member holding a code-execution permission, and inlining its output would run
  their JavaScript in the viewer's session. Read the helper's comment before changing
  anything here.
%>
<div class="contextual">
  <% if @template.editable_by?(User.current) %>
    <%= link_to reporter_dashboard_icon('edit', l(:button_edit)),
                edit_project_reporter_template_path(@project, @template),
                class: 'icon icon-edit' %>
  <% end %>
  <%# T-32 / FR-61 — the ad-hoc mail entry point.

      OFFERED ONLY WHEN THE ACTOR HOLDS THE PERMISSION, and the check is
      `mail_…_reports` alone rather than the controller's full conjunction: they are already
      reading this page, so `view_…_reports` is not in question. Drawing a link that answers
      403 is the "capability nobody can reach" defect pointed the other way.

      It is drawn for a failed run too, deliberately. The compose form is where somebody
      chooses recipients and presses send; the render happens then, not now, and a template
      that failed to draw a preview may well be fine over the scope they are about to pick. %>
  <% if User.current.allowed_to?(:mail_reporter_dashboards_reports, @project) %>
    <%= link_to reporter_dashboard_icon('email', l(:label_reporter_adhoc_mail)),
                new_project_reporter_mail_path(@project, template_id: @template.id),
                class: 'icon icon-email' %>
  <% end %>
  <%# T-28 — THE SHARE LINKS OF THIS REPORT, and the link goes to the LIST rather than
      straight to the form. §7b.1's requirement is *"you can see what you have shared… and
      take it back"*, so the page somebody lands on is the one that answers "what is out
      there", with the "new" button on it. Sending them to the form first would make
      revocation the harder of the two things to reach, which is backwards for a control.

      `share_…_reports` alone, not the controller's full set: they are already reading this
      page, so `view_…_reports` is not in question, and `publish_…` decides one FIELD on the
      form rather than access to it. %>
  <% if User.current.allowed_to?(:share_reporter_dashboards_reports, @project) %>
    <%= link_to reporter_dashboard_icon('link', l(:label_reporter_share_link_plural)),
                project_reporter_template_share_links_path(@project, @template),
                class: 'icon icon-link' %>
  <% end %>
  <%# THE DOWNLOAD IS OFFERED WHENEVER IT CAN SUCCEED, WHICH NOW INCLUDES THE ARCHIVE.
      T-29 built it (finding E-6's third bullet), so the `sections.length == 1` condition
      that used to be here — written when a multi-document export answered 501 — would now
      be hiding a feature that works. The two cases get DIFFERENT LABELS rather than one
      button with two outcomes: pressing "Download as PDF" and receiving a `.zip` is the
      same small lie §7b.3 objects to, and the count is in the label because fifty
      documents is worth knowing before the click, not after. %>
  <% if @outcome && @outcome.ok? && @outcome.sections.length == 1 %>
    <%= link_to reporter_dashboard_icon('download', l(:label_reporter_template_download_pdf)),
                document_project_reporter_template_path(@project, @template,
                                                        query_id: params[:query_id]),
                class: 'icon icon-download' %>
  <% elsif @outcome && @outcome.ok? && @outcome.sections.length > 1 %>
    <%= link_to reporter_dashboard_icon('download',
                                        l(:label_reporter_template_download_archive,
                                          count: @outcome.sections.length)),
                document_project_reporter_template_path(@project, @template,
                                                        query_id: params[:query_id]),
                class: 'icon icon-download' %>
  <% elsif @diagnostic && @template.failure_document? %>
    <%# T-30 / FR-59, AND THE UX PASS FOUND THIS. The branch above draws the download only
        when the run SUCCEEDED — so on a failed render, which is the only time a failure
        document exists, there was no button at all and the feature could be reached only
        by hand-writing the URL. A capability an administrator can switch on and nobody can
        reach is the same defect as one that is switched on and does nothing.

        It is a SEPARATE LABEL and not the same button with a different outcome: clicking
        "Download as PDF" and receiving a file called `report-FAILED-…` would be the small
        lie §7b.3 is about. The link goes to the same action, which re-runs and fails again
        — that is what makes the id on the page and the id in the document differ, and the
        panel says which one to quote for THIS attempt. %>
    <%= link_to reporter_dashboard_icon('download',
                                        l(:label_reporter_failure_document_download)),
                document_project_reporter_template_path(@project, @template,
                                                        query_id: params[:query_id]),
                class: 'icon icon-download' %>
  <% end %>
</div>

<h2><%= @template.name %></h2>

<%# §Findings S-14 — the narrowing is stated, never silent. Answers nil for an issue
    template and for an actor who sees every hour in the project, so this is rendered
    unconditionally rather than behind a branch a later edit can get wrong. %>
<% if (rrd_notice = reporter_time_entry_visibility_notice(@template, User.current, @project)) %>
  <p class="warning" id="reporter-time-entry-visibility"><%= rrd_notice %></p>
<% end %>

<%= render partial: 'degradations', locals: { outcome: @outcome } %>

<% if @diagnostic %>
  <%= render partial: 'diagnostics', locals: { diagnostic: @diagnostic } %>
<% else %>
  <%# NO TRUNCATION NOTICE HERE. `#show` runs with no issue bound, so `truncated?` is
      `total > total` — permanently false — and the copy it would print says "Preview of",
      which a report page is not. The branch was dead and the sentence would have been
      wrong if it ever fired. %>
  <% if @outcome.sections.length > 1 %>
    <p class="warning">
      <%= l(reporter_source_key(:text_reporter_template_per_record_html, @template.source),
             count: @outcome.sections.length) %>
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <% if @outcome.sections.empty? %>
    <%# A per-record template over a scope that matched nothing. An empty page with no
        sentence on it reads as a broken report; this says which of the two it is. %>
    <p class="nodata"><%= l(reporter_source_key(:text_reporter_template_no_issues,
                                                  @template.source)) %></p>
  <% end %>

  <%# T-38: the frame's own border and background are gone (chrome declares no colour),
      so the native container draws its edge. `box` on a DIV and not on the iframe —
      Redmine puts it on containers everywhere, and `.box`'s padding against the frame's
      `width: 100%` would overflow a replaced element by 22px. `preview.html.erb` already
      had this wrapper; the widget surfaces are inside `.mypage-box`, which is the same
      container under another name. %>
  <% @outcome.sections.each do |section| %>
    <div class="box"><%= reporter_report_frame(section) %></div>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
